# Git Safety Rules

## Forbidden operations (require explicit founder approval before running)

- `git push --force` or `git push -f` (force push to any branch)
- `git reset --hard` on a branch with uncommitted work
- `rm -rf` on any directory inside a repo
- `git clean -fd` without first checking `git status`
- Deleting branches via `git branch -D` (capital D forces delete)
- Modifying or deleting any file in `.git/`

## Required operations (always do these)

- Run `git status` before any destructive operation
- Confirm branch name before pushing
- Verify staged changes match intent before committing

## Branch conventions

- `brevmont-api` default branch: `master`
- `brevmont-app` default branch: `main`
- `brevmont-extension` default branch: `main`
- `brevmont-content-engine` default branch: `main`

## Commit messages

- Use conventional commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `test:`
- Imperative mood, lowercase first letter after the type prefix
- Reference the audit ticket or PR if applicable

## Pull request workflow

- Always create feature branch off latest default branch
- Run stale-branch preflight before opening PR: `git fetch && git log HEAD..origin/<default> --oneline | wc -l` should return 0
- After Cursor work, run Codex CLI review before merge (see Codex review workflow)

## Enforcement

When user asks for a destructive git operation:
1. Read `git status` first
2. Show what would be lost
3. Ask for explicit confirmation
4. Only then run

For ANY `--force`, `--hard`, or `rm -rf` request: pause, summarize the blast radius, wait for founder confirmation containing the word "yes" or "confirmed".
